# Migrating a domain to herold

This chapter describes how to move an entire mail domain onto herold: from a
small trial where a few users bridge their existing accounts in, to a complete
cutover where every mailbox lives on herold and MX points at it.

{% callout type="info" title="Status: planned" %}
The bulk migration tooling described here
(`herold admin migration-source`, `herold admin domain migrate`) is specified
in `docs/design/server/requirements/26-domain-cutover.md` (REQ-CUTOVER-\*) and
is not yet available in this build. The per-user building blocks it composes —
[External SMTP Submission](external-smtp-submission) and per-identity IMAP
import — are tracked in issue #25. This chapter documents the intended
operator workflow so the design and the manual stay in step; commands will
work as written once the feature ships.
{% /callout %}

## The journey

A typical adoption looks like this:

1. **Trial.** You own `acme.com` and run an existing mail server. You stand up
   herold on a domain you already control (a throwaway eval domain, or a
   subdomain like `mail.acme.com`), following [Going Live](going-live). A few
   users get herold accounts.
2. **Bridge.** Each trial user adds their real address `user@acme.com` to
   herold as an *identity* and configures it as an external account: external
   SMTP for sending (see [External SMTP Submission](external-smtp-submission))
   and, optionally, IMAP import so their `acme.com` mail mirrors into herold.
   They live in herold day-to-day while `acme.com` MX still points at the old
   server.
3. **Cutover.** You decide to move `acme.com` onto herold for good. You add
   `acme.com` as a hosted domain, migrate **every** mailbox from the legacy
   server, flip MX to herold, and retire the old server.

The trial and bridge steps are covered elsewhere. This chapter is about
step 3 — and specifically the two things that make it more than "create
accounts and copy mail":

- migrating the mailboxes of users who never set anything up themselves, and
- bringing the early adopters across without giving them a second account or
  losing the mail they already mirrored.

## Before you start

Add `acme.com` as a hosted domain and publish its DKIM, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT, and
SPF records **first**, exactly as in [Going Live](going-live) — but leave the
**MX pointed at the legacy server** for now. Herold can sign and send for a
hosted domain immediately; inbound keeps flowing to the old server until you
flip MX at the end. This lets you migrate while users keep working.

## Step 1 - register a migration source

You do not collect a password from every user. Instead you register one
**migration source** for the domain: a single delegated or master credential
that lets herold read any mailbox on the legacy server.

Pick the `auth_kind` that matches your legacy provider:

- `google_domain_delegation` - a Google Workspace service account with
  domain-wide delegation for the `https://mail.google.com/` scope. Herold
  mints a per-user access token impersonating each `user@acme.com`.
- `imap_master_user` - a Dovecot or Cyrus master user. Herold logs in as the
  master principal and authorises as each target user.
- `imap_admin_login` - a single admin account that is permitted to `SELECT`
  other users' mailboxes.

```
herold admin migration-source add acme.com \
    --imap-host imap.legacy.example \
    --tls implicit \
    --auth-kind imap_master_user \
    --credential @/path/to/master-secret
```

The credential is sealed at rest with herold's data key (the same mechanism as
external-submission credentials) and is only ever held in memory for the length
of a connection. Herold verifies the source can authenticate as a sample user
before accepting it. The source is admin-only and should be deleted once the
migration is done.

## Step 2 - review the plan

Migration is always plan-then-apply. Generate the plan first:

```
herold admin domain migrate acme.com --source acme.com --plan
```

For every `user@acme.com` the plan tells you whether herold will **provision**
a fresh native account or **reconcile** (re-home) an existing trial user, plus
the mailbox size estimate and any conflicts. Re-home is chosen automatically
when a trial principal already owns a verified `user@acme.com` external
identity.

Read the plan carefully. The uniqueness rule is strict: each `user@acme.com`
must resolve to exactly one account. If two trial principals both claim the
same address, herold refuses to guess and flags it for you to resolve. A trial
user who added `ceo@acme.com` as an external identity does **not** thereby own
the native `ceo@acme.com` mailbox — conversion only happens through this
reviewed step, never as a side effect of adding the domain.

## Step 3 - migrate in mirror mode

Apply the plan:

```
herold admin domain migrate acme.com --source acme.com --apply
```

Each mailbox runs the IMAP-import [complete-migration](going-live) flow: a full
backfill of the entire mailbox, deduplicated by Message-ID so nothing is copied
twice. Mailboxes migrate independently and in parallel; one slow or broken
account does not hold up the rest. Watch progress with:

```
herold imapimport status
```

For early adopters, herold **re-homes** the existing account rather than making
a new one:

- `user@acme.com` becomes the account's primary (login) address;
- the old trial address is kept as a secondary alias (use `--drop-trial-alias`
  to remove it instead);
- their already-mirrored mail, settings, filters, Sieve, labels, and the
  per-account import label all carry over - it is the same account;
- their external SMTP submission config is removed, because herold now sends
  and DKIM-signs natively for `acme.com`.

Leave the migration running in mirror mode until `herold imapimport status`
shows the backfill drained to ~0 across the batch and you have spot-checked a
few mailboxes in the suite.

## Step 4 - flip MX and finalize

Once the mirror is caught up and verified:

1. Point `acme.com` MX at herold.
2. Finalize the batch. This drives every mailbox to the `migrated` state:
   herold becomes the source of truth, and the legacy connections are retired.

Any message that arrives twice during the MX-flip window (once at the old
server, once at herold) is deduplicated, so you do not get duplicates from the
overlap.

## Rolling back

Until you finalize in step 4, the legacy server stays authoritative and
reachable, and herold never modifies anything on it. If you need to abort, stop
the migration; a mailbox that already reached `migrated` can be re-opened to
mirroring. Because herold only ever reads the legacy server, a rollback is a
herold-side operation — the old mailboxes are untouched.

## After cutover

- Delete the migration source (`herold admin migration-source delete acme.com`).
- Decommission the legacy server once you are confident.
- Early adopters keep working in the same herold account they trialled with,
  now under their real `acme.com` address.

See also [External SMTP Submission](external-smtp-submission) for the per-user
bridge that precedes a cutover, [Going Live](going-live) for domain/DKIM/MX
setup, and [Operating Herold](operate) for day-to-day monitoring.
